we've been talking about the Dakota  access pipeline where protesters have  been clashing not only with police but  with other law enforcement agencies as  well over the construction of this 1170  mile pipeline in North Dakota about 40  miles south of Bismarck is where the  majority of the protesters have been and  this involves the Standing Rock Sioux  and other Native American tribes the the  concerns are concerns that make a lot of  sense to me right fears that the  pollution of the Missouri River from  this pipeline would be a significant and  negative thing that sacred cultural  lands think about that we've given  Native American tribes these very  specifically carved out areas and then  all of a sudden fossil fuels wants to  come in and go through some of those  small areas that we've designated from  people whose country we took away from  them and I know country is not the exact  right word we're talking about land not  necessarily an organized country or  nation-state as we talk about it today  but I think you get the idea the big  news is that there are now two thousand  veterans planning to gather next week at  the reservation to serve as human  shields for protesters now why would the  protesters need human shields well  because of everything we've seen  happening to them water cannons with  cold water in sub-zero temperatures  rubber bullets hundreds of people being  taken to local hospitals assault and  intimidation at the hands of  increasingly militarized police forces  Michael Wood jr. is the founder of the  veterans group the veterans event rather  that is going to be doing this  he's a former guest on the show former  Baltimore police officer really  interesting interview with him that you  can check out and Wood said he was  hoping to attract 5,000 veterans and he  had to cut it off  when they reached 2,000 veterans because  it was just getting too big to even  manage in a sort of practical way over  half a million dollars in pledges as of  yesterday to pay for food and  transportation and supplies etc and  authorities meanwhile guys are saying we  may start finding people to block  supplies from getting in  we heard from the governor jack  Dalrymple that there's a mandatory  evacuation order saying it's because of  anticipated harsh weather conditions but  of course many people very suspicious of  that because they've been trying to  figure out a way to get these protesters  out for a very very long time so this is  continuing to escalate and I know that  some of us thought not necessarily you  guys but there were thoughts among that  some in our audience who emailed me  saying this is going to end soon and and  we're just never going to hear about it  again it doesn't appear that it's going  to end soon now or we could just see the  next stage of it it's like that old  saying where if you call the police then  I'll call the army right yeah it's great  to see veterans long after they've been  discharged continued to serve the public  because it seems like the police aren't  protecting and serving us in this case  throughout this whole debacle it kind of  seemed that the police the militarized  and the the brute force shown by the  police kind of was disproportionate to  the threat posed by the activists I'll  say yeah I don't think your assessment  that there is wrong anybody looking at  this and and we heard many lies also  from representatives for though there  was a sheriff's department or a police  department remember exactly and they  were critiqued for using these water  cannons just on protesters and freezing  cold temperatures and they said oh no  there weren't standalone water cannons  this was just hoses hooked up to fire  trucks and they were being used to put  out fires now protesters have had drones  and we played the drone footage for you  last week where you could see these were  not number one there were no fires that  were being put out by some of these  water cannons and number two they were  water cannons it was not hoses that were  hooked up to fire trucks I mean just  flat-out caught lying and they were  quite reminiscent to of the 1960s  demonstrations where protesters had  water cannons Yeah right on them and  that rallied a lot of public support  behind that cause this has been the  worst of militarization of police it's  going to continue there are lawsuits for  excessive use of force and the most  troubling thing about this what's  happening to the protesters is horrible  but the most troubling thing is this is  all over fossil fuels that we don't need  if we truly committed and obviously if  we had committed 15 years ago this would  be a non-issue but even more scary is  the connection  between this and the Trump presidency  right Trump is a shareholder in one of  the companies that stands to make money  from this pipeline Trump plans to do  massive deregulation for fossil fuel  harvesting which will make pipelines  like this even more common that to me is  you know I have personal concern for the  protesters that are being impacted by  this but the bigger picture is that  under a president Donald administration  these types of pipelines which we should  be getting away from completely are  going to be more common and I don't know  that that narrative is being discussed  enough Rachel I think that should be a  more prevalent narrative I think it is  being discussed though because people  are talking about it in the larger  context of his business holdings and  partnerships okay  yeah which do stand to get very sticky  as we move forward yeah and we're gonna  be discussing that much more tomorrow  supposed big announcements coming about  Donald Trump's uh business holdings but  it what saddest for me Pat is that  there's a conversation here that we've  been pushing for for a long time which  is hey you know what we have options for  energy where this doesn't even come up  rubber bullets don't come out if you  start moving towards alternative energy  water cannons in sub-zero temperatures  on protesters don't come out when you  get away from fracking and pipelines and  oil and and natural gas and coal etc and  it's I guess it's sort of there as  rachel is saying in in one context but  the bigger picture is we've we've really  dropped the ball on moving away from  this type of energy  yeah and it's a sad case for your cause  that that's the sort of tactics you have  to use to enforce oil and and put that  on to people instead of greener newer  technology no doubt about it so there's  probably like four or five layers here  one is militarization of police another  is the sort of total lack of respect for  supposedly sacred Native American lands  that were mostly hand-picked by by the  American government number three is the  the broader energy implications for is  the Donald Trump business interests and  there's probably five or six there as  well let's take a break send me your  thoughts about this on Twitter I'm on  Twitter at D Pakman and the show is on  Twitter at David Pakman show  and we'll be back with more after this  today's program is made possible in part  by movement watches at M VMT watches  komm I'm wearing my movement watch today  which I love I went with the white face  and the tan band which is a look I  really like and all the other watches I  have are darker and so I thought this  would be a good fit but they have so  many different washes that are really  great gifts and now that we're getting  into the holiday buying season for me  doing the entire chaos at the mall to  get a five hundred dollar watch at the  department store move that's not my move  at all  but the get a $95 watch watch is  starting at $95 at MV mt watches dot-com  / Pakman for some of the people in my  life that I'm going to be getting gifts  for that is more my move and you can get  15% off as well as free shipping as well  as free returns if you go to mV mt  watches dot-com / Pakman that is PA que  ma n

